Owner-side order data:
WFMK202607.pdf, p.17, table "Ownership of Newbuilding Orders", South Korea ro=
w:
2025 full year =3D 5,588 thousand GT (85 ships)
2026 H1 =3D 3,047 thousand GT (67 ships), UP +9.0% y/y
Annualized: 3,047 x 2 =3D 6,094 thousand GT (~6.0m GT) -- about level with 20=
25 (5.588m GT)

No Clarkson forecast exists for 2027 orders. "Flat or slightly lower" is my o=
wn call, based on 3 points below, all from World_Shipyard_Monitor_07_26.pdf:

1) Yards already booked up
Page: the very first page of the PDF (cover/highlights page - it has no "Page=
 N" footer printed on it, unlike every page after it).
Quote: "the global orderbook has continued to expand, reaching 7,612 vessels =
of 207.2m CGT at start-2H, up 19% y-o-y ... Korea has 38.3m CGT on order (18%=
) and leads in the LNG carrier sector."
Page 17, table "Orderbook by Country/Region":
- "Forward Cover" box, row "Global Total": years 2019-2026* =3D 2.5, 2.8, 3.0=
, 3.7, 3.7, 4.0, 4.1, 4.3 -- trend "UP 6% y-o-y"
- "Major Shipbuilders" table (Million GT), row "South Korea": 2025=3D65.5, 20=
26*=3D69.4; delivery columns 2026=3D9.2, 2027=3D23.1, 2028=3D23.8, 2029+=3D13=
.3

2) Newbuilding prices still high
Page 10, table "Newbuilding Price Index" (index, base Jan'88=3D100), row "Pri=
ce Index": 2018=3D130, 2019=3D130, 2020=3D126, 2021=3D154, 2022=3D162, 2023=
=3D178, 2024=3D189, 2025=3D185, Jun*=3D185, trend "STEADY -1%"
Quote (same page, commentary box): "Newbuild prices increased across Q2-26, w=
ith our Newbuilding Price Index standing at 185 points as of start-Jul, up by=
 1.7% from the recent low in Mar-26 and just 2.6% below the recent high in Se=
p-24."

3) 2026-2028 delivery wave
Page: same cover/highlights page as point 1.
Quote: "Shipyard deliveries are forecast to reach ~50m CGT in the full year (=
+13% y-o-y), before rising a further 22% to ~61m CGT in 2027 (set to exceed t=
he previous shipbuilding boom's CGT peak, though remains 5% below in dwt term=
s)."

So: 2026 pace already flat vs 2025, yards have limited free capacity (4.3-yr =
global forward cover, Korean yards' own backlog still growing), prices sittin=
g near the cycle high, and a record delivery wave lands right as 2027 orders =
would be placed -> assume 2027 lands around the 2026 annualized level (~6.0m =
GT) or a bit below.

